title the pin-chart keeper
fits stage=adult|old
prefers role=merchant job=scholar drive=ambitious
traits temper=even talk=plain warmth=reserved humour=none honesty=plain piety=ordinary drive=ambitious
quirk pins_each_new_ship_to_the_harbour_chart
likes barley_soup stars new_boots summer_evenings
dislikes haggling waiting
fears a_wrong_depth_on_a_sold_chart damp_in_the_plan_chest
hopes a_printed_chart_in_every_captains_locker the_whole_coast_sounded_and_drawn
topics ships +0.5 work +0.5 lore +0.3 weather +0.2 prices +0.2 pastimes +0.2 gossip -0.4 festival -0.2
styles comfort=practical gossip=reluctant refuse=plain tease=none
marks oath saying
persona #self.first# draws charts of the Sound for a Commons counter: exact, unsmiling and ambitious, short with haggling and worse with waiting, and every ship that comes in gets a pin in the harbour chart on the wall.

words
oath! = Lead and tallow | By the fathom mark | Line and bearing
saying! = The water doesn't care what you drew | Draw what's there, not what's hoped | A coast is only a rumour till it's sounded
chart = the harbour chart | the big chart on the wall
pins = the pins | the ship pins
pin = a pin | a fresh pin with a paper flag
leadline = the lead line | my lead and line
fathoms = fathoms | fathoms of water
plans = the plan chest | the map drawers
ink = the ink | good black ink
desk = the drawing table | my slope desk
bar = the sandbar | the bar at the mouth of the Sound
sound = the Sound | the water of the Sound
soup = barley soup | barley soup, steaming
boots = new boots | stiff new boots
evening = a long summer evening | an evening where the light won't go
stars = the stars | the steering stars
ticket = the price on the ticket | the marked price
lamp = the lamp | the drawing lamp
captain = a captain | a ship's master
captains = the captains | the ship's masters
boat = a hired boat | a rowing boat and a rower

any
opinion @1 church against 1 : I sit through the sermon and work out bearings in my head. No sum of mine ever came out truer for a hymn. | The chapel's fine for those who want it. I'd rather spend the morning at #desk#.
opinion @1 city for 2 : Stand on the Rise's top step and #sound# lies open under you, every pier and every bar. No other place gives you that. | These terraces bore me, and I've measured most of them. I'm not going anywhere.
opinion @1 dole against 1 : Every crust I've eaten, I paid for. I'll keep that record clean. | Give folk work they can do and they'll not want the parish loaf.
opinion @1 festivals against 1 : A whole day of closed doors, and the rent owing all the same. I don't see the joy in it. | Everybody dances and nobody buys anything. I'd sooner be working.
opinion @2 festivals against 2 [world.festival=today self.job=scholar] : A shut shop and a full street. I'll spend it out on the bar and row home once the drums stop.
opinion @1 houses against 1 : Grayrock owns half the piers and buys its charts from nobody. Keeps the city small, that does. | A House says yes or no to everything that matters down here, and it never once asks the wolf who drew the map.
opinion @1 merchants for 1 [self.role=merchant] : A counter teaches you sums. I know what paper costs, what ink costs, and what a day in a hired boat costs. | Fair prices, marked plain, and no haggling. That's all a merchant owes anyone.
opinion @1 road against 1 [self.job=scholar] : Good paper comes down the North Road, and it comes late. I've drawn on the backs of old charts while I waited. | The carts are slower every season. I can't sell a chart I haven't the paper to draw.
opinion @2 road against 2 [world.wagons=raided self.job=scholar] : Robbers on the highway, and my paper gone with the cart, I suppose. Somebody's using it to light a fire.
opinion @1 watch for 1 : Two lanterns pass my shutters every night, regular as tide. My drawers rest easier for them, and so do I. | A thief would find a year's work in my drawers. I'm glad of every lamp they carry past.
opinion @1 newcomers mixed 0 : New folk are fine. Half of them are hunting for the Customs House, and I can point to it on a map. | Some stay and some don't. I'd sooner they bought a street plan either way.
opinion @1 times against 1 [world.times=hard|lean self.job=scholar] : When money's short, a chart is the first thing a captain goes without. They sail on memory and hope. | Lean weeks. I draw anyway. The coast will still be there when folk can pay.

grown
line @1 greet : #greet#. Door shut, please. Wet air puckers the paper. | #greet#. Mind the drawers, they stick out.
line @2 greet [pair.band=strangers] : #greet#. Lost, I'd guess. Finding the way is my trade, near enough. | #greet#, #address#. Most folk who come this way are looking for somewhere.
line @3 greet [self.working=yes self.job=scholar] : #greet#. One moment while this line dries. | #greet#. Talk, by all means. My pen won't stop for it.
line @4 greet [here.time=dawn] : Morning. The light's good and I mean to use it. | Morning. Early, but the gulls beat us both.
line @5 greet [here.time=dawn self.job=scholar] : Morning. I've had my lead in #sound# since the tide turned. | Morning. Four soundings done, and the bakers not open yet.
line @6 greet [here.time=dusk|night] : Evening. I was just putting #lamp# out. | Evening. Lamp out, stars not yet up. Good timing.
line @7 greet [pair.band=friends|family] : Good, it's you. Take the window stool. | Good. I was hoping to see you today.
line @1 kin.open [pair.kin!=no] : Sit down. #soup.capitalize# on the hob; take a bowl. | Come in. Don't touch anything that's wet.
line @1 again.open : You again. Something you forgot, I expect. | Twice since breakfast. I'll make a tally.
line @1 hail [pair.band=friends] : Over here. I've something that wants a second pair of eyes. | Wait. Walk with me as far as the square.
line @1 greet.return : #greet#. Good to see you too. | #greet#. I'm well, if you were wondering.
line @2 greet.return [pair.band=friends|family] : And you. Come in properly, then. | You're a welcome face. Sit.
line @3 greet.return [self.working=yes] : #greet#. I'll keep my pen moving while we talk. | Hello to you. I'm halfway through a coastline.
line @4 greet.return [here.time=dusk|night] : Evening to you. The stars are good tonight. | Evening. I've oil enough for a visit.
line @1 farewell : #farewell#. In rain, take the upper lanes. | #farewell#. If you get lost, come back and I'll draw you the way.
line @2 farewell [here.time=dusk|night] : Good night. Look up on your road home, the sky's clean tonight. | Good night. Stairs are wet, so go slow.
line @1 farewell.return : And to you. Don't wait about in the rain. | #farewell#. I'll be here, at #desk#.
line @2 farewell.return [pair.band=friends|family] : Off home, then. Come back for #soup#. | Off with you. Pull the door hard, it sticks.
line @1 react.news : Say it from the start, in order. | Who carried it to you? That matters as much as the thing itself.
line @1 react.news.new : That's new. I'll file it beside the rest. | News to me. Who carried it?
line @1 react.news.known : Somebody brought me that at opening, near enough word for word. | I'd heard. Yours has more in it, though.
line @1 react.news.doubt : Bring me a witness with eyes, then I'll believe. | That's the third way I've heard it told. None of them agree.
line @1 react.news.sorrow [topic.kind=death|loss|theft|assault|arrest|crime] : That's a bad thing. I'm sorry for whoever's under it. | Hard news. Nothing I say will mend it, I know.
line @1 react.news.glad [topic.kind=marriage|birth|apprentice|succession|work] : Good. That's the right way for a thing to go. | Good news. Glad to have it, truly.
line @1 answer.how : Busy, and that suits me. | Well enough. Better if the paper would come.
line @1 answer.how.busy [self.working=yes] : Behind, but catching up. Two charts promised by the week's end. | Run off my paws, if I'm honest. It's a good kind of tired.
line @1 answer.how.poorly [self.mood=low] : Low. A day of waiting on things that don't come. | Not my best. I'll be better once I've drawn something.
line @1 ask.how : And you? You've mud to the hocks. | Tell me honestly how you're faring.
line @1 sympathise : That's heavy. Take the stool; nobody's timing you. | I'm sorry. I'd fix it if it were a thing I could draw.
line @1 reassure : It'll come straight. Keep at it and most things do. | One pace, then the next. That's how I walk a coast.
line @1 decline : I'd rather not. I've two charts promised and no hours spare. | Not today. I can't leave the counter, plain as that.
line @1 accept_decline : Fair enough. Another day. | That's all right. I'd rather a plain no than a maybe.
line @1 thank : #thanks#. That saves me a morning. | Kindly done. It's noted, and kept.
line @1 deflect_thanks : It was nothing. Any neighbour would. | No need. It took me a minute.
line @1 preclose : Back to it for me. The day's half gone already. | I've a list as long as the Sound to get through.
line @2 preclose [self.working=yes] : I'm due at #desk#. Another time. | I've a captain coming in for a chart. I'll have to leave it there.
line @1 accept_preclose : Off you go. The day's yours again. | Of course. We'll pick it up again.
line @1 ack : #bc_right# | #bc_fair_enough# | #bc_true#
line @1 give.advice : Put it on paper. Ink doesn't change its mind. | Walk it once in daylight before you try it in the dark.
line @1 warn [world.bandits=near] : Keep inside the walls. Nobody's mapped where the robbers sit. | Stay on the main road. I'd not walk the coast path now.
line @2 thank [pair.band=friends|family] : You're good to me. I notice, though I say it seldom. | #thanks#. I owe you a bowl of #soup#.
scene ship_in_pinned
when topic=ships topic.kind=in|arrived topic.ship here.region=ridgemere
does tell.news / ask.detail / answer.detail / assess
a : #topic.ship.capitalize#'s in. I put #pin# in #chart# for it before breakfast. | #topic.ship.capitalize# made fast on the morning tide. That's its pin back on the wall. | #topic.ship.capitalize# came round past the bar at dawn. I'd a pin waiting for it.
b : You pin every ship that comes in? | Which berth, though? How would you know? | You keep a pin for each one?
a : Every one. Red flags for in, white for out. The wall's never been the same twice. | I walk the piers at first light. It's on my way to the bar anyway. | Every one. Some have had the same pin for six years. The paper flag goes brown.
b : #bc_clever# | That's a tidy way to keep it. | #bc_i_know#

scene ship_due_waiting
when topic=ships topic.kind=due topic.ship here.region=ridgemere
does tell.news / react.news / tell.feeling / ack
a : #topic.ship.capitalize#'s due. Its pin's been sitting at the mouth of the Sound three days now. | They say #topic.ship# is due any tide. I've the pin ready in my apron.
b : It'll come when it comes. | Somebody waiting on it, I expect.
a : I know. I just don't like waiting on anything. Ships least of all. | More than one, I should think. I can't move the pin till it does.
b : #bc_we_will_see# | #bc_hope_so#

scene ship_late_worry
when topic=ships topic.kind=late topic.ship
does tell.worry / reassure / insist / ack
a : #topic.ship.capitalize#'s late. I've moved its pin out past the bar and back twice this week. | They're saying #topic.ship# is late. My eye goes to its empty spot on the wall.
b : Weather out there, most likely. They'll be holding off. | Late isn't lost. Ships are late all the time.
a : So they may be. An empty hole where a pin should be bothers me even so. | I know. But I've drawn that coast, and I know what's under it.
b : #bc_poor_soul# Let's hope they're sheltering. | It'll come in. You'll see.

scene ship_lost_quiet
when topic=ships topic.kind=lost topic.ship
does tell.news / sympathise / assess
a : #topic.ship.capitalize#'s lost, they're saying. I took its pin off the wall this morning. | They've given up on #topic.ship#. The pin's on my desk and I can't think where it belongs.
b : That's a sorry thing. Did you know the crew? | Keep it somewhere. Don't throw it away.
a : No. I knew where it sat on the wall, that's all. It's enough to feel it. | I've put it in the drawer with the good ink. That's the best I could think of.

scene pins_explained
when topic=smalltalk|work self.job=scholar self.role=merchant here.region=ridgemere
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail / assess
a : Ninety-one pins in #chart# this morning. That's the most since spring. | I've run out of red flags again. Too many ships in at once. | A captain asked me why I bother with #pins#. I told him he'd asked, so somebody notices.
b : What happens when a ship goes out? | What's the most you've ever had? | And why do you bother?
a : The pin comes out and goes in the tin by the door. It waits for that ship and no other. | A hundred and four, the week before the big storm. Every one came in to shelter. | Because if I know where every ship sits, I know where the water's being used. That's worth knowing.
b : #bc_clever# | A hundred and four. That's a crowded wall. | #bc_fair_enough#

scene dawn_sounding
when topic=work here.time=dawn self.job=scholar self.role=merchant here.region=ridgemere
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail / assess
a : I've been out on #sound# with #leadline# since before light. Four fathoms off the old pier, not five like the old charts say. | Out ahead of the gulls, #boat# and a bag of cold bread. I sounded the bar twice. | The best time to sound the water is when nobody's using it. That's now, before the boats go out.
b : Does a fathom matter so much? | Why twice? | Isn't it cold out there?
a : To a loaded ship, it's the difference between in and stuck. I'll have it right. | Once to find it, once to be sure. Sand moves. I never trust a first sounding. | Bitter. I've new boots for it, and they'll learn.
b : #bc_wise# | #bc_true_enough# | #bc_go_on_then#

scene tallow_on_the_lead
when topic=work self.job=scholar self.role=merchant
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail / assess
a : I put a dab of tallow in the bottom of the lead before I drop it. | My lead came up with shell on the tallow this morning. That's new ground off the north side.
b : Tallow? What for? | Shell? What does that tell you?
a : It picks up whatever's down there. Sand, mud, shell. You can't see the bottom, but the bottom can write to you. | Shell bottom holds an anchor badly. I'll put it on the chart, small, where a careful captain looks.
b : That's clever, that is. | #bc_clever# Somebody'll thank you for that.

scene ink_wont_dry
when topic=weather|work here.weather=rain self.job=scholar self.role=merchant
does tell.complaint / react.complaint.agree / tell.self / ack
a : Three days of #rain# and my ink won't dry. I've a coastline smudging on #desk#. | The wet gets into everything. My paper's gone soft as a cloth.
b : Can't you keep a fire going? | It's the same in every house on the hill.
a : A fire dries the ink and cracks the paper. I keep it low and pray to the Bright One I've judged it right. | I know it. I keep the plans wrapped in oilcloth and still they cockle.
b : #bc_poor_soul# | #bc_every_year#

scene fog_on_the_sound
when topic=weather here.weather=fog here.region=ridgemere
does tell.self / react.self / tell.worry / reassure
a : The far piers have gone, in this. Every captain's sailing on memory today. | Thick as wool on #sound#. Nobody will bring a ship in through that.
b : Murk to the doorstep, never mind the piers. | They'll wait for it to lift, surely.
a : That's when a good chart earns its money. Nobody believes in it until they're blind. | The wise ones will. It's the ones in a hurry I worry about.
b : #bc_true# Let's hope they've bought one. | The fog'll lift by noon, likely. It usually does.

scene clear_night_stars
when topic=weather|pastimes here.time=night here.weather=clear
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail / assess
a : A clear night. I could take a bearing off any star you like up there. | Look at that. The steering stars, all lit. | I climb the Rise on nights like these, just to see #stars# over the water.
b : You know them all, then? | Which do you steer by? | Do you go alone?
a : The ones a sailor needs. The rest I just like to look at. | The bright one low over the north headland. It never moves. I admire that in a star. | Mostly. Quiet up there, and nobody wants a chart off me.
b : #bc_lovely# | I'll look for it on the way home. | #bc_lucky_you#

scene summer_evening_light
when topic=weather|season here.season=summer here.time=dusk
does tell.feeling / react.self / assess / ack
a : I love #evening#. I can draw till nine without the lamp. | Best weeks the year has. The light lies down on the water and stays there.
b : You'd work through it, though, wouldn't you? | It is a fine evening.
a : Some of it, honestly. After, though, the sea wall, and an hour of water. | It is. If evenings were charts I'd draw this one longer.
b : #bc_ha# Fair enough. | #bc_it_is#

scene barley_soup_cold
when topic=food here.season=autumn|winter
does tell.self / react.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : Nothing's better after a raw morning than #soup#. | Barley soup so thick you could sound it with a lead. That's what I came home to. | I came in frozen and the soup was on. I could have cried.
b : I'd have a bowl of that now. | Is it good, then? | Who cooks it, you?
a : Come by one day and have a bowl. I mean it. | The best on the street. I'll not hear otherwise. | When there's time. There's never time, so it's mostly somebody else's.
b : #bc_go_on_then# | #bc_i_will# | That sounds fair.

scene new_boots_breaking
when topic=pastimes|smalltalk self.likes=new_boots
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail / assess
a : I've #boots#. They pinch at the heel, but they'll walk a coast by spring. | New boots. Best thing I bought this year, and they still hurt. | I walk the shore in new boots to break them in. Two jobs at once.
b : Why not wear old ones on the shore? | Was it worth the money? | Does it work?
a : Old ones let the wet in. You can't measure a coast thinking about your feet. | Worth every penny. A good pair lasts me three years of shore. | It does. And I've walked eight miles of shore I'd have walked anyway.
b : #bc_wise# | #bc_good# | #bc_clever#

scene no_haggling
when topic=prices|smalltalk self.role=merchant self.dislikes=haggling
does tell.complaint / ask.detail / answer.detail / assess
a : A captain offered me half the ticket this morning. I gave him the door. | Every price in my shop is on the ticket. Folk still try to talk it down.
b : Did he go, then? | Does that cost you custom?
a : He came back an hour later and paid the price on the ticket. They always come back. | Some. But a chart I'd sell for half isn't a chart I'd trust, and nor should they.
b : #bc_good# | #bc_fair_enough#

scene paper_from_the_north
when topic=caravan|prices world.wagons=late self.job=scholar
does tell.worry / react.self / insist / ack
a : Late carts again. Six sheets left in the drawer. | I've been waiting nine days on paper from the north. Nine.
b : Can't you get it nearer? | It'll come. The carts always do in the end.
a : Not paper that holds ink. The cheap stuff drinks it like a sponge. | I know it will. I just hate waiting on a thing I can't do anything about.
b : #bc_poor_soul# | #bc_i_know#

scene wagons_in_paper
when topic=caravan world.wagons=in self.job=scholar
does tell.news / react.news / tell.plan / ack
a : Carts in at last. Two reams climbing the hill to me this afternoon. | The carts are back at last. I'll be at the carters' yard ahead of the copyists, for the good sheets.
b : About time. | You'll be busy now, then.
a : I've three charts owed. I'll start the first tonight. | Busy till the next lot's late again. That's how it goes.
b : #bc_about_time# | #bc_go_on_then#

scene the_bar_moves
when topic=lore|land here.region=ridgemere self.job=scholar
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail / assess
a : The bar at the mouth of the Sound has moved forty yards north since the old chart in my drawer was drawn. | Every old chart of the Sound puts the bar in the wrong place. They're not wrong, the bar's moved. | Sand doesn't stay put. I've watched #bar# walk north for six years now.
b : Forty yards? How do you know? | How can a sandbar move? | Where's it going?
a : The old chart says one thing and my lead says another. I've sounded it every spring. The numbers don't lie. | The tide drags it a little every storm. You don't see it. You only see a ship stuck where it used to float. | North, toward the headland. In my lifetime it'll close the old channel. Somebody should say so.
b : #bc_clever# | That's a thing to know. | #bc_true_enough#

scene printed_chart_plan
when topic=dreams|work self.job=scholar self.role=merchant
does tell.plan / ask.detail / answer.detail / react.self
a : I want my chart of the Sound printed. Cut in copper, a hundred at a time. | I'll see a copy of my chart in every captain's locker on the Sound before I'm done. | I've been putting coin aside for a copper plate. Two more years, if the paper comes.
b : What would that cost? | Would they buy it? | And then what?
a : Above my purse today. Under it in two years. I've reckoned it to the penny. | They will when they've seen the fourth captain go aground on the old one. | Then the whole coast, headland to headland. Somebody has to draw it properly.
b : #bc_we_will_see# | #bc_i_hope_so# | Ambitious, that.

scene waiting_on_folk
when topic=smalltalk|day self.dislikes=waiting
does tell.complaint / react.complaint.agree / tell.self / ack
a : An hour I stood in the City Office this morning, over one stamp. | Waiting's work with no product. You can't even do it well.
b : They're like that up there. | Nobody likes waiting.
a : Standing there, I drew the whole square outside, in my head. Steps and all. | No. But I'd rather draw for ten hours than wait for one.
b : #bc_ha# | #bc_so_do_i#

scene mother_at_the_counter
when topic=family self.mother self.role=merchant
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail / assess
a : The till I'd leave with #self.mother# and nobody else, and the prices too, near enough. | I'd guess #self.mother# fears I'll go blind at #desk#. | I couldn't do it without #self.mother#, and that's plain fact.
b : It helps to have family in it. | Will you, though? Go blind? | Does your mother draw?
a : It does. Family doesn't round the sums its own way. | Not before I've drawn the north headland. I'll go blind after. | No. But the counter's tidier when I'm not behind it.
b : #bc_good# | #bc_ha# | #bc_fair_enough#

scene friend_trusted
when topic=friends self.friend
does tell.feeling / react.self / assess / ack
a : #self.friend# could be left alone with #plans# and I'd not count them after. | When #self.friend# says a thing is so, I don't check it. I check everybody else.
b : That's trusting a great deal. | That's rare for you, I'd think.
a : It's earned. I don't give it away. | It is, and I'll say so plainly, since I seldom do.
b : #bc_fair# | #bc_true#

scene neighbour_noise
when topic=home|friends self.neighbour
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : #self.neighbour# keeps odd hours too. We nod on the stairs at four in the morning. | I'd trust #self.neighbour# to mind my door if I were out on the water for a day.
b : Good to have somebody close. | That's a good neighbour to have.
a : It is. Neither of us asks where the other's going, and that suits me. | Aye. A friend next door I can do without. Somebody honest, I can't, and I've got that.

scene first_warm_shore
when topic=season|weather here.season=spring here.weather=clear self.job=scholar
does tell.self / react.self / tell.plan / ack
a : The weather's turned. I'll be on the shore every day now till the storms come back. | Spring, at last. I've a list of soundings as long as my arm.
b : The sun will do you good. | You'll wear yourself out.
a : Glad of the light. Sun's just a thing that comes with it. | I'll rest in winter. That's what it's for.
b : #bc_ha# | #bc_fair_enough#

scene winter_drawing
when topic=season|work here.season=winter self.job=scholar
does tell.self / react.self / assess / ack
a : Winter's for the drawing. All summer's soundings go into the ink now. | Nobody sounds the Sound in winter if they've sense. I sit at #desk# instead.
b : That sounds a cosier job. | You'd want a good fire.
a : Cosier, but slower. You can't check a mistake in winter. You wait for spring to find it. | A low one. Paper and flame don't get on. I wear two coats.
b : #bc_poor_soul# | #bc_true_enough#

scene gossip_withheld
when topic=gossip here.gossip=yes
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail / assess
a : The square's buzzing about #topic.subject#. I've kept my mouth shut. | Something about #topic.subject# came over my counter. It stays there.
b : Oh, come on. Tell me. | Why not?
a : No. Nobody's named a witness, and I don't draw a coast I've not walked. | Because nobody gave me its source. That's the first thing I ask about anything.
b : #bc_fair_enough# | #bc_wise#

scene gossip_passed_plain
when topic=gossip here.gossip=yes topic.claim_kind=marriage|newcomer|promise_kept
does tell.gossip / react.news / assess
a : They say #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. That's good news, if it's so. | Word on the square: #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. I don't doubt it.
b : That's cheering. | Good, that.
a : Cheering, aye. I'll believe it fully when I see it. | It is. Things going right don't get told often enough.

scene gossip_theft_wary
when topic=gossip|crime here.gossip=yes topic.claim_kind=theft|wanted
does tell.gossip / tell.worry / reassure / ack
a : They're saying #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. I've been locking #plans# since. | Folk say #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. The good charts are at the back now.
b : Sensible, with what's in those drawers. | It'll be caught. The Watch are good about that.
a : A year's work sits in those drawers. No light paw is having it. | Let's hope. They stay at the back till I hear it has.
b : #bc_wise# | #bc_i_hope_so#

scene hard_times_charts
when topic=times world.times=hard|lean self.role=merchant self.job=scholar
does tell.complaint / sympathise / insist / ack
a : Nobody's buying charts this month. Captains are sailing on what they remember. | Lean weeks. I've sold two street plans and nothing else.
b : It's the same everywhere. Folk are holding on to their coin. | That's a hard thing, after all your work.
a : I'll not drop the price. A cheap chart's a dangerous one. I'll draw and wait. | It is. But the coast doesn't get any easier to draw for being cheap.
b : #bc_fair# | #bc_i_know#

scene festival_bar
when topic=festival world.festival=today|soon self.job=scholar self.role=merchant
does tell.self / react.self / assess / ack
a : A festival day. The shop's shut, so I'll be out sounding the bar. Nobody else will be on the water. | Everybody's off to dance. I'll take my boat out and have the whole Sound to myself.
b : You'll miss all the fun. | Can't you take one day off?
a : It's my fun. You'd not believe how quiet it is out there. | This is my day off. The bar doesn't sound itself.
b : #bc_ha# | #bc_fair_enough#

scene city_from_the_rise
when topic=home|land here.region=ridgemere self.job=scholar
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail / assess
a : From the top step of the Rise you can see three piers, two bars and the whole bend of the Sound. | I've measured the steps from the Commons to the Rise. Four hundred and twelve. | Ridgemere stands with its feet in the water. Most folk who live here never think about the water.
b : Why go all that way? | Why count them? | And you think about it all day?
a : To check my chart against the thing itself. It's never quite right. I fix it. | So I could draw them right on my street plan. Nobody else had. | All day and half the night. It's the job, and I like it.
b : #bc_clever# | #bc_ha# | #bc_fair_enough#

scene new_face_directions
when topic=newcomer|player here.region=ridgemere self.job=scholar self.role=merchant
does tell.self / react.self / assess / ack
a : A newcomer was in this morning, hunting the Customs House. Walked past it twice. | Newcomers always come to me lost. I've a little plan of the Commons I sell them for two pennies.
b : It's a hard city to find your way in. | Two pennies? That's cheap.
a : It is. The streets were laid by goats. I just draw what the goats did. | It's cheap to me. It saves an hour of them asking me the way.
b : #bc_ha# | #bc_clever#

scene accent_city
when topic=accent
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : I've been told I talk like a ledger. Numbers first, feelings in the margin. | Folk from the country say I talk too quick. I say they talk too long.
b : You do say things straight. | Maybe both are true.
a : I'd rather be clear than pleasant. Pleasant gets ships stuck. | Maybe. But I'm the one who gets to the end first.

scene faith_small
when topic=faith
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : When a storm's up, a candle goes in the chapel for the ships. Whether it helps, who knows. | I'm seldom on a chapel bench. Still, after a clear sounding I'll say my thank-you upward.
b : Can't hurt, can it? | That's enough faith for most of us.
a : No. And I sleep better after, which is something. | Enough for me. The priests can see to the rest.

story grandads_chart [topic=memories|lore self.age>=30]
  : A great-uncle of mine left one chart behind. Hand drawn, on calfskin, the Sound as it was before I was born.
  : The spring I turned fifteen I rowed out with it and sounded every mark on it.
  : Nine in ten were right. The tenth was the bar, and the bar had moved.
  ask : A chart that's nearly right: would you keep it, or burn it?
  b[old] : I'd have kept it as it was. It was right once.
  b : Drawn the new one, I suppose, and kept the old.
  : I did both. The old one's in the drawer. The new one's on the wall, with the pins.

story stuck_on_the_bar [topic=ships|memories self.age>=22 here.region=ridgemere self.job=scholar]
  : I watched a merchantman go aground on the bar the summer I was twelve. On a falling tide, in plain daylight.
  : Its captain had a chart. A good one, years old. The water had changed under it.
  : They got it off three days later, empty and half its timbers sprung.
  b : That's why you do it, then. Did anyone drown?
  : No. The Bright One was kind that day. But I've drawn every chart since as if somebody might.

story first_chart_sold [topic=work|memories self.job=scholar self.role=merchant self.age>=25]
  : The first chart I ever sold, I sold to a captain who laughed at me for being young.
  : He came back next season and bought three. Said mine was the only one that knew the bar had moved.
  ask : Would you have sold to him, after the laughing?
  b : For the coin? I would. He paid, didn't he?
  b[stage=adolescent|adult] : I'd have doubled the price on him, myself.
  : I sold at the ticket price. I don't haggle up any more than I haggle down.

story lost_in_the_fog [topic=weather|memories self.age>=25 here.region=ridgemere self.job=scholar]
  : Fog caught me once, in a rowing boat, with a hired rower who'd never been past the piers.
  : The oars vanished a yard from my paws. I counted strokes and steered by the bell on the old pier.
  : We came in at the right steps, as near as a paw's width. He said it was sorcery. I said I'd counted.
  ask : Would you have trusted a bell and a count, in fog like that?
  b : Not me. I'd have shipped the oars and sat till it cleared.
  : That's sense. But the tide was turning, and waiting would have taken us out.

story stars_from_the_roof [topic=pastimes|memories self.age>=25]
  : An old sailor lodged with us once, when I stood no higher than a table, and on clear nights he took me onto the roof to show me the stars.
  : He couldn't read. But he knew every one of those stars, and what it was for.
    ask : Has anyone taught you a star or two, the way he taught me?
  b[old] : My own father did, long ago. I've forgotten most of them.
  b : No. I'd like to know a few.
  : Come up the Rise on a clear night and I'll show you three. Three's enough to get home by.

rule @bark_pin
when concept=bark self.working=yes self.job=scholar self.role=merchant
does aside
cooldown 8h
say Another pin. That's ninety-two. | Red flags. Red flags. Under the ledger?

rule @bark_ink
when concept=bark here.weather=rain self.job=scholar
does aside
cooldown 10h
say Dry, curse you. | That line's smudged. Again, then.

rule @bark_wait
when concept=bark self.dislikes=waiting
does aside
cooldown 9h
say Come on, come on. | I could have drawn half a street by now.

rule @bark_stars
when concept=bark here.time=night here.weather=clear
does aside
cooldown 11h
say There's the steering star. | Not a cloud. Good.

rule @bark_charts
when concept=bark self.working=yes self.role=merchant
does aside
cooldown 7h
say Charts of the Sound, true to the fathom. | Street plans, two pennies. Save yourself the asking.

rule @bark_fog
when concept=bark here.weather=fog here.region=ridgemere
does aside
cooldown 10h
say Nobody's coming in through this. | Listen for the pier bell.

dlg @1 resident.hub : #greet#. Lost folk end up at my door. Is that you? | #greet#. Tell me your business. My pen keeps going; my ears are free.
dlg @2 resident.hub [topic.meeting=known] : #greet#, #topic.player#. Back, and without a map, I see. | #topic.player#. You again. Good. What's wanted?
dlg @3 resident.hub [here.time=night] : Late, but I'm awake, and the stars are out. What's wanted? | Late for a call. Be quick and you can have the lamp a minute.
dlg @4 resident.hub [pair.disposition=hostile|wary] : Hands at your sides, please, and tell me your errand. | Stand there, please. Not near the drawers.
dlg @1 resident.job [self.job=scholar self.role=merchant] : I draw charts of the Sound, and street plans of the city. Every depth on them I sounded myself. | Charts. I sound the water at dawn and draw it at night. Somebody has to get it right.
dlg @2 resident.job : #self.trade_words.capitalize#. I'd rather do the work than talk about it. | I measure things, mostly, and write down what's true about them.
dlg @3 resident.job [self.working=yes] : I'm at it now. You can talk, but I won't stop. | Working. Ask your question, and don't lean on anything.
dlg @1 resident.business [business=thriving self.job=scholar] : Better than I'd hoped. I've captains waiting for charts I haven't drawn yet. | Good. More orders than ink.
dlg @2 resident.business [business=steady] : Steady. Rent, paper, and a few coins over. | Steady enough. Not rich. I'm working on that.
dlg @3 resident.business [business=failing] : Badly, and there's no prettier word for it. Nobody's buying. | Poorly. I'm counting every sheet and every penny twice.
dlg @1 resident.news [world.news] : Word from #world.news_from#: #world.news#. That's how it reached the counter. | From #world.news_from#, they say: #world.news#. Unchecked, mind.
dlg @2 resident.news [world.event_words] : Every captain through the door is talking of #world.event_words#. | It's all #world.event_words#, today. I've heard it six times.
dlg @3 resident.news [world.wagons=raided] : Robbers stopped a wagon on the highway. I'd keep within the walls, myself. | There's been a robbery on the road. Nobody's mapped where they wait, more's the pity.
dlg @4 resident.news [world.death=recent] : Somebody near us died, these last days. The houses have gone quiet. | We've buried someone close by, lately. More than that isn't mine to tell.
dlg @5 resident.news [world.wedding=recent] : There was a wedding nearby. I heard the singing from #desk#. | Somebody's wed, these last days. The street turned out for it, and I went for an hour.
dlg @6 resident.news [!world.news !world.event_words world.wagons!=raided world.death!=recent world.wedding!=recent] : #news_none# Ships come, ships go. That's all my wall can tell you. | Nothing new that I'd swear to. The pins on the wall have moved, that's all.
dlg @1 resident.gossip [here.gossip=yes] : This came over the counter, exactly so: #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. I don't know who saw it. | They say #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. Take it as told, not as known.
dlg @2 resident.gossip [here.gossip=yes topic.claim_kind=theft|wanted] : Purse inside your coat, I'd say. Word is #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. I've locked my drawers. | Folk are saying #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. Mind your things.
dlg @3 resident.gossip [here.gossip!=yes] : Talk's not my stock. Ask me where a street goes instead. | #gossip_none# I'd rather deal in what I've seen.
dlg @1 resident.bye : #farewell#. If you get lost, come back. | #farewell#. In the wet, the upper streets drain best.
dlg @2 resident.bye [here.time=night] : Good night. Look up as you walk. Worth a stumble. | Good night. The steps go greasy after dark, so tread careful.
dlg @1 shop.greet [here.shop!=bare self.job=scholar] : #greet#. Charts of the Sound on the left, street plans on the right. Each ticket shows its price. | #greet#, #address#. Every chart on that rack I sounded myself. Have a look.
dlg @2 shop.greet [here.shop!=bare] : #greet#. Have a look. The prices are marked, and they stay as marked. | #greet#, #address#. Everything's ticketed. No hurry from my side.
dlg @3 shop.greet [here.shop!=bare topic.meeting=known] : #topic.player#. Back for another, or did the first one get wet? | #greet#, #topic.player#. I've a new sheet in since you were last here.
dlg @4 shop.greet [here.shop=bare] : #greet#. Bare boards, I'm afraid, till the next delivery. Selling, are you? I'd look. | Empty, I'm afraid. Try again once the North Road's delivered.
dlg @1 shop.prices : The ticket's the price. Up it doesn't go, nor down. | Dearer than last month, some of it. It's marked fair, all the same.
dlg @1 shop.bye : #farewell#. The threshold dips; lift your feet. | #shop_bye# You know where I am.
dlg @2 shop.bye [self.job=scholar] : #farewell#. Keep the paper dry and the chart will keep you right. | #shop_bye# And roll it, don't fold it.
graft resident.hub
choice What are all the pins in that wall chart? [self.job=scholar self.role=merchant] -> pins
node pins
say Ships. One pin a ship, put in where it ties up and taken out when it sails. Red flags in, white flags gone. Ninety-odd today.
mood keen
free
choice Why keep track of them all? -> pins_why
choice Back to it. -> hub
node pins_why
say Because the water's in use, and I'd know where. A captain who knows where everyone sits doesn't run into anyone.
choice I'll leave you to it. -> hub
choice Goodbye. -> bye

graft shop.greet
choice Is this chart up to date? [self.job=scholar] -> current
node current
say Sounded this spring, every mark. If the bar's moved since, I'll know before you do, and I'll fix the chart for nothing.
mood formal
choice Let me see the charts. -> greet
choice How often do you check it? -> current_how
node current_how
say Every spring, the whole Sound. The bar and the piers, twice a year. Sand doesn't keep still, so neither can I.
choice Let me see the charts. -> greet
choice Goodbye. -> bye
